# G018 — Count Vowels

*v2. See `count-vowels.md` for prose: first measurement of content
(content → value), frequency distribution as first statistical
operation, include-y as context-dependent classification decided by
agent role, vowel-ratio as derived metric (micro-`where`).*

---

## Choreographic Case: Content Quality Analysis

[content-quality @text
    IN ORDER TO
        measure several properties of @text concurrently
        combine them into a readability score
        and pass only if the combined score clears a threshold

    concurrent [
        readability: @vowels <- [count-vowels
                                   {text: @text, include-y: false}]
    |
        complexity:  @wl     <- [word-lengths {text: @text}]
    |
        density:     @sl     <- [sentence-lengths {text: @text}]
    ]
    join

    @score <- [calc {expr: "(@vowels:ratio * 100) - " +
                             "(@wl:mean * 5) - " +
                             "(@sl:mean * 2)"}]

    @score
        where at least 50 -> #readable
        otherwise          -> #too-dense
]

---

## Verb Surface

[count-vowels
    {text, include-y}                         ;; -> Int
]

[vowel-breakdown
    {text, include-y}                          ;; -> {vowel → count}
]

[vowel-ratio
    {text, include-y}                          ;; -> Float 0..1
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: context-dependent classification `where` role decides

[count-vowels {text: @t, include-y: @inc-y}]
    IN ORDER TO
        count y as a vowel only when the caller's frame demands it
        because "my" and "yes" treat y differently and no universal
        answer exists outside the caller's role

    targets <- @inc-y ? [a, e, i, o, u, y] : [a, e, i, o, u]
    count codepoints of @t that are in targets
]

A phonetician always includes y; a spellchecker never does. The
`where` doesn't resolve the ambiguity; the agent's role does. The
include-y flag is the resolver's way of surfacing that role choice.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: breakdown as frequency distribution

[vowel-breakdown {text: @t, include-y: @y}]
    partition codepoints of @t by vowel-identity
    count per partition
    emit {vowel → count}
    IN ORDER TO
        generalise to every "measure distribution across elements"
        pattern the noosphere needs (word freq, topic mix, tag
        distribution) from the minimal vowel case
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [count-vowels {text: "Hello World", include-y: false}]
    ;; -> 3

    -> [vowel-breakdown {text: "Hello World", include-y: false}]
    ;; -> {e: 1, o: 2}

    -> [vowel-ratio {text: "Hello World", include-y: false}]
    ;; -> 0.30  (3 vowels / 10 letters)
]
